    "final_answer": "I now have comprehensive information about the passive voice from the Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section. Let me provide a complete summary of what I found.\n\n---\n\n# Passive Voice in Grammar — Cambridge Dictionary\n\nI successfully found multiple Grammar pages on the Cambridge Dictionary website that explain the passive voice. The Grammar section is organized under: **Grammar > Verbs > Passive voice**, with several sub-pages.\n\n## 📄 Page 1: Passive: active and passive\n\n**URL:** https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british/verbs/passive_voice\n\n**Explanation:**\n\"We use the terms active voice and passive voice to talk about ways of organising the content of a clause:\"\n\n**Examples:**\n- **Active:** *Cambridge University Press published this book.*\n- **Passive:** *This book was published by Cambridge University Press.*\n\n\"The active voice is the typical word order. We put the subject (the topic or the theme) first. The subject is the 'doer' or agent of the verb:\"\n- **Active:** *Edward Barnes designed these houses in the 1880s.*\n\n\"In the passive, the person or thing that the action happens to comes first. We use 'by' + 'doer' (agent):\"\n- **Passive:** *These houses were designed in the 1880s.*\n- **Passive + agent:** *These houses were designed in the 1880s by Edward Barnes.*\n\n## 📄 Page 2: Passive: forms\n\n**URL:** https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british/verbs/passive_voice_forms\n\n**Explanation:**\n\"The most common passive structure is be + -ed form:\"\n\n**Examples:**\n- **Active:** *Five million people watch the show every week.* (active present simple of *watch*)\n- **Passive:** *The show is watched by five million people every week.* (passive present simple of be + -ed form of *watch*)\n\n**Forms of the passive in different tenses:**\n\n| Tense | Be form | Example |\n|-------|---------|---------|\n| present simple | am / are / is + -ed | *Am I invited too? One or two new types of insect are found in Britain each year.* |\n| present continuous | am/are/is + being + -ed | *Am I being recorded? We're already being served, thanks. That computer isn't being used any more.* |\n| past simple | was / were + -ed | *The story was reported in yesterday's paper. They weren't labelled.* |\n| past continuous | was/were + being + -ed | *Was he being examined? They were being watched carefully.* |\n| present perfect simple | have/has + been + -ed | *Haven't they been asked to the wedding? He's been hurt.* |\n| past perfect simple | had + been + -ed | *An important discovery had been made. Had he been injured in the war?* |\n| modal simple | will/might + be + -ed | *I don't think I'll ever be paid. Might we not be allowed to go in?* |\n| modal continuous | could/may/must + be + being + -ed | *She couldn't still be being interviewed. The photocopier may be being repaired.* |\n| modal perfect simple | could/must + have been + -ed | *Do you think we could have been heard? The document must have been written.* |\n\n**Additional note:**\n\"We use passive forms of tenses in the same way as active forms, but we need to remember:\"\n- **Active:** *Mr Lloyd and Mrs James teach Geography.* (present simple active)\n- **Passive:** *Geography is taught by Mr Lloyd and Mrs James.* (present simple passive)\n\n## 📄 Page 3: Get passive\n\n**URL:** https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british/verbs/get_passive\n\n**Explanation:**\n\"We form the standard passive with be + -ed form. We form the get passive with get + -ed form:\"\n\n**Examples:**\n- **Standard passive:** *The trees in the garden were damaged in the wind.*\n- **Get passive:** *The trees in the garden got damaged in the wind.*\n\n\"We use the get passive especially in informal speaking. When we use the get passive, we also place a little more emphasis on the nature of the action itself or on the person involved in the action:\"\n- *There were blizzards over night and the whole town got snowed in.*\n- *I've got good news for you. Jim finally got promoted.*\n- *They had only known each other for six months when they got divorced.*\n\n\"The get passive is commonly used in speaking of events that happen to people:\"\n- *They've worked there for three years but they still got fired.* (lost their jobs)\n\n\"We sometimes use a reflexive pronoun with the get passive. This suggests the subject was at least partly responsible for the event or made it happen:\"\n- *I got myself locked out the other day. I stupidly left my keys in the bedroom.*\n\n---\n\n## Related pages also available on the site:\n- **Have something done**\n- **Passives with and without an agent**\n- **Passive: uses**\n- **Passive: other forms**\n- **Passive: typical errors**\n\n✅ **Task complete** — I successfully navigated the Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section and gathered comprehensive explanations and examples of the passive voice across three dedicated pages.",
